.TH RMUT 1 "July 2026" "rmut 1.28.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rmut \- a mutt-like terminal mail client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rmut
.RB [ \-R ]
.RI [ MAILDIR " | " MBOX " | " imap:ACCOUNT [/ FOLDER ]]
.br
.B rmut \-\-import\-muttrc
.RI [ MUTTRC ]
.br
.B rmut
.B \-V
|
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rmut
is a terminal mail client with mutt keybindings, built in Rust.
It reads maildirs, mbox files and IMAP folders, sends through
sendmail(1) or SMTP, threads messages, filters them with mutt-style
patterns, and signs/encrypts with gpg(1).
.PP
Without an argument it opens the first mailbox from
.B mail.mailboxes
in the configuration, then
.BR $MAIL ,
then
.IR ~/Maildir .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-R
Open the mailbox read-only: nothing is ever written, not even read
marks; delete, flag, and sync refuse with a note, and the status
line's %r shows a %.
.TP
.BI \-\-import\-muttrc " \fR[\fPMUTTRC\fR]\fP"
Translate a muttrc (default
.I ~/.muttrc
or
.IR ~/.mutt/muttrc )
into rmut TOML on stdout, for review and saving as the configuration.
Directives with no rmut equivalent are kept as comments.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a short usage summary.
.SH MAILBOXES
.TP
.I MAILDIR
A maildir path. Flag changes and deletions sync on
.BR $ .
.TP
.I MBOX
An mbox file such as
.IR /var/mail/$USER .
The file is mirrored into a cache maildir;
.B $
rewrites the mbox in place under an exclusive flock (a backup copy is
kept in the cache during the rewrite) and refuses when the spool
changed since the mirror \(em refresh with
.B G
and sync again.
.TP
.IB imap: ACCOUNT [/ FOLDER ]
An IMAP folder of a configured
.B [[accounts]]
entry (INBOX when
.I FOLDER
is omitted), over TLS with local caching.
.B $
maps flag changes and deletions back to the server. Dead connections
reconnect transparently; new mail arrives via IDLE or the regular
poll.
.SH KEYS
The bindings below are the defaults; the
.B [keys]
configuration remaps them, macros may shadow them, and
.B ?
always shows the live keymap.
.SS Index
.TP 12
.B j k Up Down
next / previous message
.TP
.B PgUp PgDn Ctrl+B Ctrl+F
page up / down
.TP
.B = *
first / last message (also Home / End)
.TP
.B Enter
view message
.TP
.B d u
mark for deletion / unmark
.TP
.B F
toggle flagged mark
.TP
.B N
toggle read/unread
.TP
.B t ;
toggle the tag; apply the next function to tagged messages
.TP
.B Tab Alt+Tab
jump to the next / previous new-or-unread message, wrapping around
(mutt's next-new-then-unread)
.TP
.B D U T Ctrl+T
prompt for a pattern and delete / undelete / tag / untag every
matching message (within the active limit), reporting the count
.TP
.B $
write changes to the mailbox
.TP
.B q x
quit (asks before purging deletions) / quit without saving. Leaving
a mailbox ages unread new mail to old \(em shown as O, no longer
counted as new (mutt's mark_old).
.TP
.B m r g f
compose, reply, reply to all, forward
.TP
.B e Alt+e b
edit the raw message (mutt's edit; the changed result replaces the
original); edit as a new draft (resend); bounce to new recipients
.TP
.B o
choose sort order
.TP
.B l /  n
limit by pattern; search by pattern; repeat last search
.TP
.B c y
open a mailbox by path; browse nearby mailboxes. At the path prompt
Tab completes the configured mailboxes, the open account's folders
and nearby maildirs (repeated Tab cycles); Tab on an empty prompt
opens the folder browser. The save/copy prompts complete the same
way. Inside the browser
.B c
prompts for any directory to browse, Enter opens a maildir or
descends into a plain one (`..' goes up), and
.B C
creates a maildir (relative paths land in the browsed directory).
.TP
.B G
check for new mail now
.TP
.B v
list message parts (attachments); in that menu Enter views a text
part (or one with a matching [filters] entry),
.B s
saves,
.B |
pipes the decoded part to a shell command, and
.B p
prints it via print
.TP
.B Alt+v Alt+V
fold/unfold current thread; fold/unfold all threads
.TP
.B s C
save (copy and mark deleted) / copy to a mailbox
.TP
.B | p
pipe raw message to a shell command; pipe to the print command
.TP
.B a
add the sender to the alias file
.TP
.B Q
look up addresses with query_command: the matches list in a menu,
Enter starts a message to the picked address
.TP
.B X
notmuch search: the query goes to
.B notmuch search \-\-output=files
and the hits open as a virtual read-only mailbox (symlinks into the
cache) \(em view, reply, copy, and pipe work there; flag changes
and deletes refuse, since the real copies live elsewhere. Keep the
database fresh with "notmuch new" in a delivery hook or cron;
disable the key with [mail] notmuch = false
.TP
.B B Ctrl+N Ctrl+P Ctrl+O
toggle the sidebar; move its highlight; open the highlighted mailbox
.SS Pager
.TP 12
.B q i Esc
back to the index
.TP
.B j k Enter Backspace
scroll one line
.TP
.B Space \-
page down / up (also PgDn / PgUp, Home / End for top / bottom);
Space past the end opens the next message (mutt's pager_stop
default), and wrapped lines carry a leading + (mutt's markers)
.TP
.B Ctrl+D Ctrl+U
scroll half a page down / up
.TP
.B T S
show/hide quoted text; skip past the quoted text below. Quoted
lines are tinted by depth (see quote_regexp and the quoted colors);
search hits are highlighted
.TP
.B J K
open next / previous message
.TP
.B d
delete and advance
.TP
.B h
toggle full headers
.TP
.B / n N
search the displayed text (case-insensitive, regex like the
patterns; unlike the index /, which matches whole messages);
.B n
repeats forward,
.B N
backwards, both wrap around with a note
.PP
.BR v ", " m ", " r ", " g ", " f ", " p ", " s ", " C ", " | ,
.BR b ", " e " and " a
work as in the index.
.SS Prompts
Every line prompt is a small line editor: Left/Right, Home/End (also
ctrl+a / ctrl+e), Backspace and Del (ctrl+d) around the cursor,
ctrl+u kills to the start, ctrl+k to the end, ctrl+w the word before
the cursor. Up/Down recall the prompt kind's history (patterns,
addresses, mailboxes, files, commands), newest first.
.SS Compose questions
Like mutt's ask-yes defaults: replying to a message with a Reply-To
asks "Reply to ...?" (no falls back to From), an empty Subject asks
"No subject, abort?", and replies ask "Include message in reply?"
before quoting the original. Enter takes the default (yes).
.SS Compose menu
After the editor, the draft lands in mutt's compose menu: the header
block (From/To/Cc/Bcc/Subject/Fcc/Security) above the attachment
table (the body, a forwarded original, every Attach: file with size
and type).
.B y
sends,
.B e
reopens the editor,
.B Enter
views the selected entry (text directly, other types through their
[filters] command),
.BR t / c / b / s
edit To/Cc/Bcc/Subject (address prompts Tab-complete),
.B a
attaches a file,
.B D
detaches the selected file
.RB ( j / k
move),
.B p
opens the PGP menu (encrypt/sign/both/clear),
.B P
postpones, and
.B q
asks "Postpone this message?" \(em no discards. Recalling with
several postponed drafts offers a picker.
.SH PATTERNS
Limit and search take mutt-style patterns:
.PP
.nf
  ~f x  from       ~s x  subject     ~b x  body
  ~t x  to         ~c x  cc          ~C x  to or cc
  ~e x  sender     ~d spec  date     word  subject or from
  ~N new   ~U unread   ~F flagged   ~D deleted   ~T tagged
  ~p addressed to me
.fi
.PP
.I x
is a case-insensitive regex; "quotes" keep spaces. Date specs:
.BR 24/12/2026 ,
.BR 1/6/2026\-30/6/2026 ,
.BR 24/12\- ,
.BR <1w ", " >2d ", " =3d .
Terms AND;
.B !
negates,
.B |
ORs,
.B ()
groups:
.B !~D (~f jane | ~t jane) ~d <1m
.SH CONFIGURATION
TOML, read from
.B $RMUT_CONFIG
or
.IR ~/.config/rmut/config.toml .
A missing file means defaults; a broken file is ignored with a
warning. All sections and keys are optional.
.SS [identity]
.TP
.B name, email
The From identity.
.TP
.B reverse_name
A reply's From becomes whichever of your addresses the original was
sent to (mutt's reverse_name).
.SS [[identities]]
Conditional identities, applied in order over
.B [identity]
and the account identity \(em the minimal folder-/send-hook. Keys:
.B folder
(glob on the open mailbox, path or imap: spec),
.B recipient
(glob on any draft recipient),
.BR name ", " email .
With both globs set both must match; unset name/email keep the value
from the layer below.
.SS [mail]
.TP
.B mailboxes
Mailboxes for the folder browser, new-mail watching and the sidebar;
the first is the default mailbox.
.TP
.B sent, postponed
Mailboxes receiving sent copies and postponed drafts.
.TP
.B sendmail
The delivery command (default
.IR /usr/sbin/sendmail );
SMTP is used instead for imap: mailboxes with an smtp_host.
.TP
.B editor
Draft editor; default $VISUAL, $EDITOR, then vi.
.TP
.B poll_seconds
New-mail poll interval.
.TP
.B print
Command the printed message is piped to (default lpr).
.TP
.B save
Default target offered by
.BR s .
.TP
.B forward
"inline" (quoted text, the default) or "attach" (the original goes
along as a message/rfc822 part, mutt's mime_forward).
.TP
.B query_command
External address lookup for Tab completion at the To prompt (%s is
the word, or it is appended), e.g. "khard email \-\-parsable %s".
.TP
.B trash
Purged messages move here (a maildir path or imap:account/folder of
the open account) instead of being erased; purging inside the trash
deletes for real (mutt's $trash).
.TP
.B edit_headers
mutt's edit_headers (default false, like mutt): true puts the
draft's header block (From/To/Cc/Subject, Attach: lines) into the
editor buffer for direct editing. Off, the prompts set To and
Subject, and attachments go through the compose menu's
.BR a .
.SS [index]
.TP
.B format
The index line, see
.B FORMAT STRINGS
below.
.TP
.B sort
Initial sort: date, from, subject, size or threads, with an optional
"reverse\-" prefix.
.TP
.B sort_aux
"last\-date\-sent" orders threads by their newest message.
.TP
.B date_format
chrono strftime string for the date column (default "%b %e", like
mutt's index date).
.SS [pager]
.TP
.B index_lines
Lines of the message index kept visible above the pager.
.TP
.B context
Lines of overlap when paging (mutt's pager_context).
.TP
.B quote_regexp
Regex classifying quoted body lines (mutt's $quote_regexp; default
"^([ \\t]*[|>:}#])+"). The depth \(em the number of quote characters
in the match \(em picks the quoted color.
.TP
.B ignore unignore hdr_order
mutt's header weeding for the brief view: lists of header-name
prefixes ("*" matches all); ignored headers are hidden unless a
prefix in unignore re-includes them, and hdr_order sorts the shown
ones (unlisted names follow in message order). Unset, the classic
view shows Date/From/To/Cc/Subject. The h key still toggles the
full list.
.TP
.B format
mutt's $pager_format for the pager's bottom line: %C message
number, %m count, %n sender, %s subject, %Z status characters,
%P percent through the message, %f mailbox, plus the conditional
and %>X machinery. Default: "\-\-\-Message %C/%m: %s \-\- %P".
.TP
.B wrap
mutt's $wrap: wrap body text at N columns instead of the window
width; a negative N leaves a right margin of |N|.
.TP
.B tilde
mutt's $tilde: pad the rows below end-of-message with ~.
.SS [ui]
.TP
.B theme
"default" or "mono".
.TP
.B status_format
The bottom status line, see
.B FORMAT STRINGS
below.
.SS [sidebar]
.TP
.B visible, width
The left mailbox pane (toggle at runtime with
.BR B ).
.SS [colors]
Slot overrides: status_fg, status_bg, deleted, flagged, header.
Quoted text tints by depth with quoted, quoted1, quoted2, ...
(default: cyan for every depth); pager search hits use search_fg /
search_bg (default: reverse video).
.SS [[color_index]]
Pattern rules for index lines:
.B pattern
plus
.B fg
and/or
.BR bg .
The first matching rule wins over the slot colors; broken rules warn
and drop.
.SS [[color_body]]
Regex rules for pager body spans (mutt's `color body`):
.B pattern
is a plain regex (not a message pattern) plus
.B fg
and/or
.BR bg ,
applied in order to every match on a displayed line \(em URLs, diff
lines, and the like.
.SS [filters]
MIME type \(-> shell command rendering the part (stdin to stdout),
e.g. "text/html" = "w3m \-dump \-T text/html". Used when a message
has no text/plain part, and in the attachment viewer.
.SS [keys.index], [keys.pager]
Remaps,
.IR action " = " key ,
e.g. sync = "w". Action names appear in the
.B ?
help; keys are a single character,
.BR ctrl+x / alt+x ,
or a name: enter, esc, space, tab, backspace, up, down, pgup, pgdn,
home, end.
.SS [macros.index], [macros.pager]
.IR key " = " sequence
\(em the key replays the sequence (literal characters plus key names
in angle brackets: <enter>, <esc>, <ctrl+x>, ...). Macros feed the
input queue, so they can drive prompts, and shadow a binding on the
same key.
.SS [[accounts]]
.TP
.B name, user
The account, opened as imap:name/FOLDER.
.TP
.B password_command
Shell command whose first stdout line is the password (pass(1)-style;
wins over
.BR password ).
.TP
.B password
Plaintext password \(em keep the file at mode 600.
.TP
.B imap_host, imap_port, imap_tls
IMAP server; port 993 with TLS from the first byte by default,
STARTTLS on any other port.
.TP
.B smtp_host, smtp_port, smtp_tls
SMTP submission; port 587 with STARTTLS by default, implicit TLS on
port 465.
.TP
.B auth, token_command
"password" (default), "xoauth2" or "oauthbearer"; the OAuth
mechanisms authenticate with a fresh access token from
.B token_command
(refresh is that tool's business \(em oauth2ms, mutt_oauth2.py).
.TP
.B sent_folder
IMAP folder receiving the Fcc copy of sent mail (default Sent).
.TP
.B identity
From identity when composing from this account's mailboxes, e.g.
identity = { name = "Jane Work", email = "jane@work.example.com" }.
.SS [pgp]
.TP
.B command
The gpg executable (default gpg). Decrypt/verify happens
automatically when a viewed message is PGP; signing and encrypting
are chosen in the compose menu (p). Passphrases are gpg-agent's business
\(em rmut never sees them.
.TP
.B sign_key
Signing key for \-\-local\-user; gpg's default key when unset.
.TP
.B sign_by_default, encrypt_by_default
Preselect signing/encrypting for new drafts.
.SH FORMAT STRINGS
.B index.format
takes
.BI % [\-][ min ][. max ] X
specifiers:
.B \-
left-aligns,
.I min
pads,
.I .max
truncates.
.B %C
number,
.B %Z
status/flag/mark characters,
.B %d
date,
.B %F
from,
.B %L
list-aware from ("To <list>" for List-Id mail),
.B %c
size,
.B %l
body lines,
.B %M
messages hidden under a collapsed thread,
.B %s
subject,
.B %%
a literal percent. Mutt conditionals work:
.B %?X?then&else?
renders
.I then
when field X is set and non-zero. The default matches mutt's:
"%4C %Z %\-6d %\-15.15L (%?l?%4l&%4c?) %s".
.PP
.B ui.status_format
shares the syntax with these specifiers:
.B %f
mailbox,
.B %m
messages,
.B %M
shown-when-limited,
.B %n
new,
.B %u
unread,
.B %d
deleted,
.B %F
flagged,
.B %t
tagged,
.B %s
sort,
.B %V
limit pattern,
.B %r
mailbox mark (* pending changes, % read-only),
.B %P
index position (all/top/bot/NN%),
.B %v
version. A single
.B %>X
pushes everything after it to the right edge, filling the gap with
X, so mutt's default status line renders as-is. The default
reproduces the classic line:
"\-\-\-rmut%r: %f [Msgs:%?M?%M/?%m New:%n%?d? Del:%d?] (sort:%s)%?V? (limit:%V)?".
.SH MUTTRC IMPORT
.B rmut \-\-import\-muttrc
prints a translated configuration to stdout:
.PP
.nf
  rmut \-\-import\-muttrc > ~/.config/rmut/config.toml
.fi
.PP
It understands identities, mailboxes, sendmail, editor, IMAP/SMTP
accounts (including imap_authenticators/smtp_authenticators for
OAuth), aliases, bindings, macros made of plain keys, color index
rules, index_format, status_format, trash and more. Anything without
an rmut equivalent is kept as a comment for review; stored passwords
next to an OAuth setup are redacted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/rmut/config.toml
The configuration
.RB ( $RMUT_CONFIG
overrides).
.TP
.I ~/.config/rmut/aliases
Mutt-style alias file, lines of "alias nick address..."
.RB ( $RMUT_ALIASES
overrides).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/rmut/
IMAP caches, mbox mirrors and the header cache
.RB ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME
overrides the parent).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RMUT_CONFIG, RMUT_ALIASES
Alternate configuration and alias files.
.TP
.B MAIL
Fallback mailbox when none is configured.
.TP
.B VISUAL, EDITOR
Draft editor when
.B mail.editor
is unset.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mutt (1),
.BR sendmail (8),
.BR gpg (1),
.BR pass (1)
